Turn the frame loop around: let UIKit call the frame.

0 A.D. starts, the GL context stands, the config is read, the frame loop is
reached -- and about four seconds later:

    EXC_BAD_ACCESS, KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS at 0x110
      libdispatch  <- __CFRunLoopRun <- CFRunLoopRunSpecific
      <- UIKit_PumpEvents <- SDL_PumpEventsInternal <- SDL_WaitEventTimeout
      <- PumpEvents() / Frame() / RunGameOrAtlas()      main.cpp:762
      <- SDL_main                                       main.cpp:842
      <- -[SDLUIKitDelegate postFinishLaunch]
      <- __NSFireDelayedPerform <- __CFRunLoopDoTimer <- __CFRunLoopRun

Read from the bottom: SDL calls SDL_main from a run-loop *timer callback*,
0 A.D. never gives control back, and SDL_PollEvent then enters that same run
loop a second time from inside the callback. Nothing else here is unusual; the
nesting is. Switching SDL's iOS pump off was tried first as a measurement: the
crash disappeared and nothing rendered any more, which settled it.

So SDL_main hands the frame to UIKit and returns. SDL's delegate is built for
exactly this -- it deliberately does not exit when SDL_main returns ("to support
applications that do setup in their main function and then allow the Cocoa event
loop to run"), and it switches its own event pump off at that moment, so
SDL_PollEvent inside the frame no longer enters the run loop at all. Touches
still arrive: UIKit delivers them to SDL's view controller, which queues them.

The price is that the frame outlives SDL_main, while on every other platform
what it touches are locals of RunGameOrAtlas: the log file, the GUI script
interface, the RL interface. Those move into one session object that is created
once and never destroyed -- iOS ends the process, it does not unwind it. For the
same reason the shutdown in main() is skipped while the game is still running.

The restart path (do/while over ShutdownType::Restart, used after installing a
mod) is not taken on iOS: the callback unregisters itself instead. Non-visual
runs keep the old loop, having neither a window to hang a frame on nor a run
loop to nest in.

--- source/main.cpp
+++ source/main.cpp
@@ -555,6 +555,54 @@
 
 // moved into a helper function to ensure args is destroyed before
 // exit(), which may result in a memory leak.
+#if OS_IOS
+namespace
+{
+/**
+ * What has to outlive SDL_main on iOS.
+ *
+ * UIKit owns the run loop. SDL calls SDL_main from a run-loop timer callback
+ * (-[SDLUIKitDelegate postFinishLaunch]), so a frame loop that never returns
+ * runs *inside* that callback -- and SDL_PollEvent then enters the same run
+ * loop a second time from in there. Four seconds of that and libdispatch
+ * dereferences a null pointer. Switching SDL's pump off made the crash go away
+ * and nothing render, which is what settled the diagnosis.
+ *
+ * So the loop is turned around: SDL_main registers the frame with UIKit and
+ * returns, and each frame then starts *from* the run loop. SDL's delegate is
+ * built for this -- it deliberately does not exit when SDL_main returns, and it
+ * switches its own event pump off at that point, so nothing nests any more.
+ *
+ * What the frame needs are locals of RunGameOrAtlas on every other platform.
+ * Here they live in one object that is created once and never destroyed: iOS
+ * ends the process, it does not unwind it.
+ */
+struct IosSession
+{
+	FileLogger logger;
+	std::optional<ScriptInterface> guiScriptInterface;
+	std::optional<RL::Interface> rlInterface;
+	int fixedFrameFrequency{0};
+};
+
+IosSession* g_IosSession{nullptr};
+
+void IosFrameCallback(void*)
+{
+	if (g_Shutdown != ShutdownType::None)
+	{
+		// The desktop would unwind here and possibly restart. On iOS the system
+		// ends the process; carrying on would leave UIKit calling into an
+		// engine that is shutting itself down.
+		SDL_iPhoneSetAnimationCallback(g_VideoMode.GetWindow(), 1, nullptr, nullptr);
+		return;
+	}
+	Frame(g_IosSession->rlInterface ? &*g_IosSession->rlInterface : nullptr,
+		g_IosSession->fixedFrameFrequency);
+}
+}
+#endif // OS_IOS
+
 static void RunGameOrAtlas(const std::span<const char* const> argv)
 {
 	const CmdLineArgs args(argv);
